WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.000
Over the past decade, none of Batman's growing&nbsp;
family of friends have proven quite so exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.360
as Batwoman — especially now that she has&nbsp;
finally made her small screen debut. But just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:17.440
who is Batwoman? Here's a look at the untold truth&nbsp;
behind one of DC's most fascinating characters.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:26.880
Kathy Kane, the original Batwoman, debuted in&nbsp;
Detective Comics #233 in July 1956. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:33.200
the character wasn't introduced by DC out of any&nbsp;
real desire to expand Batman's universe. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.480
Kathy Kane was concocted to combat some&nbsp;
scurrilous rumors about the Caped Crusader.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.240
In 1954, the psychiatrist Fredric Wertham&nbsp;
had published Seduction of the Innocent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.200
a highly dubious yet influential treatise&nbsp;
on the dangers of superhero comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.440
In this book, Wertham had this to say&nbsp;
about Batman and Robin's relationship:

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.200
"It is like a wish dream of two&nbsp;
homosexuals living together."

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.880
"Holy Romeo and Juliet!"

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.520
Wertham's book would go on to spark&nbsp;
full-blown congressional hearings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.360
and devastate the world of genre fiction. And&nbsp;
while most superhero titles escaped unscathed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.120
Batman's editors decided that they&nbsp;
needed to restore the appearance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.160
heteronormativity to Gotham City.&nbsp;
Thus: Kathy Kane, the Batwoman.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.680
As a wealthy socialite and former circus performer&nbsp;
whose admiration for Batman drove her to a life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:29.520
of crime fighting, Kathy was a love interest in&nbsp;
the style of Superman's ever-popular Lois Lane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.200
Of course, Bruce Wayne has a thing&nbsp;
for her, but she only has eyes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.120
Batman — and to complicate matters, he knows&nbsp;
her secret identity… but she doesn't know his.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.640
This dynamic produced some fairly&nbsp;
interesting early adventures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.320
and even introduced a sidekick in&nbsp;
the form of Kathy's niece Betty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.360
the original Bat-Girl — who was a similarly&nbsp;
positioned love interest for Robin. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.320
though, Kathy proved to be more popular with&nbsp;
readers than she was with incoming Batman editor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:02.160
Julius Schwartz, who pared down the Batman family&nbsp;
dramatically when he took over in the early '60s.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.160
While Kathy Kane was eventually phased out of the&nbsp;
comics, the idea of keeping a woman in the Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.560
family persisted elsewhere — specifically, on&nbsp;
television. In 1967, at the height of the Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.080
TV show's popularity, the producers decided to&nbsp;
expand their cast and introduce a new Batgirl.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.280
"Glad to have you flanking us, Batgirl."&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.560
The TV show tied the new Batgirl to an existing&nbsp;
character by introducing her as Barbara Gordon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.280
the daughter of Gotham City's solemn and&nbsp;
stentorian police commissioner. The viewers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.680
clued in on her secret identity, but unlike Kathy&nbsp;
Kane, Batman and Robin initially had no idea who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.520
she really was. Batgirl proved to be a reliably&nbsp;
popular member of the TV show's Bat-family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.520
and, soon enough, Barbara Gordon&nbsp;
was introduced in the comics, too.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.400
The comic book version of Barbara was just&nbsp;
as popular with fans as her TV counterpart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.080
As a result, the character served as Batgirl&nbsp;
until 1988, and then stepped back into the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:06.160
from 2011 to the present. Even when she wasn't&nbsp;
Batgirl, Barbara regularly featured as Oracle, an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:11.520
incredibly skilled hacker who often provided vital&nbsp;
information to Batman and the Justice League.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.400
When Batman Family launched in 1975, the second&nbsp;
issue asked readers to write in with their picks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.840
for which secondary Batman characters they wanted&nbsp;
to see featured alongside Batgirl and Robin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.720
Apparently, the response was strong enough that&nbsp;
they featured a reprint of an early Batwoman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.480
story in the next issue. By then, the fan letters&nbsp;
were demanding a proper comeback for Kathy Kane.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.520
In #10, these readers finally got their wish when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.600
Batwoman came out of retirement for&nbsp;
a team-up story with her successor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.240
Batgirl. At the end of the story, Kathy&nbsp;
offers Barbara the title of "Batwoman."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:51.840
Babs refuses, however, claiming that while it&nbsp;
was an honor, there can be "only one Batwoman."

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.160
Soon enough, though, there'd be no&nbsp;
Batwomen at all. Four years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:02.560
in Detective Comics #485, Batwoman is killed&nbsp;
by the League of Assassins. That was the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.080
readers saw of the main universe's Kathy Kane&nbsp;
for nearly 30 years, and in the mid-'80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.827
Crisis on Infinite Earths rearranged the&nbsp;
timeline so that she'd never existed at all.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.640
In 1999, Barbara Gordon was busy being&nbsp;
Oracle and Kathy Kane had been written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.040
out of existence — so there was nothing&nbsp;
stopping DC from teasing a potential new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.800
female Bat character in the pages&nbsp;
of the No Man's Land story arc.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.760
No Man's Land follows the heroes and villains&nbsp;
of Gotham in the aftermath of a devastating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.800
earthquake, which leaves the entire city as&nbsp;
little more than a post-apocalyptic ruin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.920
All but abandoned by the U.S. Government, Gotham&nbsp;
becomes a battleground for the countless gangs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.680
supervillains who are vying for domination. What's&nbsp;
worse, Batman has been missing for three months…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.800
so, naturally, someone puts on a bat costume&nbsp;
and starts claiming territory in his name.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.520
This particular Batwoman turns out to be the&nbsp;
Huntress, but she isn't the only one to wear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.000
the distinctive costume during No Man's&nbsp;
Land. At the end of the story, the cowl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.440
is passed on to Cassandra Cain, a teenager who&nbsp;
was trained since birth to become an assassin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.320
With the Huntress back in her normal identity,&nbsp;
Cassandra decides to become the new Batgirl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.440
leaving the door open for an&nbsp;
all-new Batwoman to appear.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:22.560
In 2006, the DC Universe jumped forward by a&nbsp;
year, and then explained everything in a year-long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.400
weekly miniseries called 52. The series set&nbsp;
the stage for plenty of major developments, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.080
most prominent and long-lasting of which was the&nbsp;
introduction of the all-new Batwoman, Kate Kane.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.280
Not to be confused with the&nbsp;
Silver Age's Kathy Kane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.840
Kate sported a brand-new black-and-red&nbsp;
costume, designed by Alex Ross.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:47.120
She also made headlines after she was introduced&nbsp;
as DC's highest-profile lesbian character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.600
especially once she took over Detective&nbsp;
Comics as its headlining star.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.960
While Kate used Batman's iconography in order&nbsp;
to strike fear into Gotham City's criminals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:01.120
she was dramatically different from the&nbsp;
Caped Crusader. She had military training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.000
was significantly less averse to firearms,&nbsp;
and her equivalent to Alfred was her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.560
a retired general who provided her with&nbsp;
experimental military hardware. There was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.640
however, one key similarity between Kate and&nbsp;
Bruce: she too was driven to fight crime after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.120
seeing her family killed in front of her —&nbsp;
in this case, her mother and twin sister.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:28.080
Detective Comics #858 dives into Kate Kane's&nbsp;
origin story, and the following issue deals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:32.640
with her dismissal from the United States&nbsp;
Military Academy during her senior year.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:38.640
While training at West Point, Kate is accused of&nbsp;
being in violation of Article 125 of the Uniform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:44.240
Military Code of Justice. Just in case you're not&nbsp;
totally up to speed on the specifics of military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:50.000
law, that's the article concerning homosexuality,&nbsp;
which was once used to keep LGBTQ people out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.480
the military. While Kate is offered the chance&nbsp;
to claim that it was all a misunderstanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.840
she instead chooses to leave West Point and never&nbsp;
fulfill her lifelong dream of serving in the Army.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.960
Naturally, that sense of integrity has been&nbsp;
one of her defining characteristics ever since.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.880
It's worth noting that this story&nbsp;
was originally published in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.760
when the official policy of the military was&nbsp;
"Don't Ask, Don't Tell" — meaning that gay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.360
people could serve, just as long as they were&nbsp;
in the closet. Only two years later, in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:25.440
that policy was changed, and Article 125&nbsp;
was officially repealed from the UCMJ.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.920
That means that, eventually, Kate's&nbsp;
origin may have to be retconned. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.480
considering this would be a fictional retcon&nbsp;
that comes at the cost of real-world progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.200
it's no surprise that the creators of that&nbsp;
origin story are absolutely fine with it.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.320
When DC rebooted their entire&nbsp;
line in 2011 as "The New 52,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:50.640
Batwoman got her first ongoing series, co-written&nbsp;
by J.H. Williams III and William Haden Blackman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.360
The most notable addition to Batwoman's roster&nbsp;
was Maggie Sawyer, who first met Kate during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.320
her headlining run in Detective Comics, and&nbsp;
quickly became the character's love interest.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.560
Maggie was originally introduced in the&nbsp;
Superman books of the '90s as a police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.600
officer in the Metropolis Special Crimes Unit.&nbsp;
Back then, she was one of the most prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:15.440
gay characters in comics, playing a major role&nbsp;
in Superman's ongoing saga. In the early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.360
she was transferred to Gotham City's&nbsp;
police department in Gotham Central,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.440
a book that, not coincidentally,&nbsp;
was co-written by Greg Rucka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:29.040
one of Kate's creators. The relationship between&nbsp;
Maggie and Kate became a huge part of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.480
and led to a groundbreaking moment in&nbsp;
Batwoman #17 — when Kate proposes to Maggie.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:39.120
Unfortunately, the marriage never came to be.&nbsp;
The editors at DC forbade the wedding from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.400
taking place, a controversial decision that&nbsp;
prompted Williams and Blackman to walk out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:50.480
in protest. DC insisted that the decision&nbsp;
wasn't made as a result of an anti-LGBTQ agenda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.400
but rather because of an editorial shift&nbsp;
that turned against marriage in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.520
with publisher Dan DiDio saying that, quote,&nbsp;
"heroes shouldn't have happy personal lives."

00:09:01.095 --> 00:09:03.760
"And what should we call you? Rain? Miss Parade?"

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.160
"Kane. Kate Kane."

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:12.880
Over the last decade or so, the CW has formed a&nbsp;
full-on DC multiverse on television, with a core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:19.040
roster featuring some of the most iconic DC heroes&nbsp;
of all time. Occasionally, the "Arrowverse'' shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:24.960
would crossover in massive team-up events.&nbsp;
One of these was the Elseworlds story in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:30.560
which introduced a brand new character to the&nbsp;
Arrowverse: Batwoman, as portrayed by Ruby Rose.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.560
The CW's take on Batwoman exists in a Gotham City&nbsp;
where Batman disappeared three years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:41.360
leading to a surge in crime and the need for&nbsp;
a new, bat-themed vigilante crime-fighter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.800
In September 2019, the fully-fledged Batwoman&nbsp;
series debuted with Rose at the forefront,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.320
and The CW ordered a second&nbsp;
season just a few weeks later.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.480
Playing a major comic book character on screen&nbsp;
could be a career-defining move for almost any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:01.600
actor, and, for a while, it looked like Ruby&nbsp;
Rose would follow that upward trajectory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:07.600
However, in May 2020, just a few months after&nbsp;
the CW asked for a second season of Batwoman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.760
Rose announced that she would be departing&nbsp;
the series. In a statement, she said:

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:16.480
"This was not a decision I made lightly&nbsp;
as I have the utmost respect for the cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.200
crew, and everyone involved with the show. Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.200
you to everyone who made season one&nbsp;
a success — I am truly grateful."

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:28.320
So why would an actor walk away from a plum&nbsp;
role and a regular gig on network television?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.200
Variety later reported that Rose had a deep&nbsp;
distaste for the series' long shooting days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:39.360
which led to tension on the set. In the end, a&nbsp;
mutual decision was made between Rose, the CW,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.313
and Warner Brothers to find a&nbsp;
new actor to portray Batwoman.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.120
In the same news reports announcing Ruby Rose's&nbsp;
exit from Batwoman, the show's production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:53.840
company Warner Brothers made it clear that this&nbsp;
wasn't going to spell the end for the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:58.960
Within days of Rose's departure, it was announced&nbsp;
that Kate Kane would also be cut from the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.560
in favor of a new character&nbsp;
who uses the Batwoman alias.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.640
A leaked casting call revealed that&nbsp;
producers were looking for an actor, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:12.240
"Female, Mid-late 20s, any ethnicity" to&nbsp;
portray Ryan Wilder, the new Batwoman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:18.000
who is supposed to be, quote, "likable, messy,&nbsp;
a little goofy," and "nothing like Kate Kane."

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.200
However, show runner Caroline Dries almost&nbsp;
took the easier route of finding a new actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:28.240
to play Kate Kane. In an interview at&nbsp;
the ATX Festival At Home, she explained:

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.320
"I did consider the 'soap opera version'&nbsp;
for a hot minute, because selfishly we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:36.880
already had a couple episodes written,&nbsp;
and transition-wise it would be seamless."

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:42.400
Ultimately, Arrowverse overseer Greg Berlanti&nbsp;
made the decision to introduce a new character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:47.680
along with a fresh actor. In the end, the new&nbsp;
Batwoman was revealed to be Javicia Leslie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:51.520
known for her roles on the BET&nbsp;
crime show The Family Business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:56.000
and the CBS drama God Friended Me. Her&nbsp;
first appearance on Batwoman came in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.840
January 2021 episode titled, appropriately&nbsp;
enough, "Whatever Happened to Kate Kane?"

